# PENDING WORKFLOW CHANGE for #1834 — apply and delete this file.
#
#     git apply scripts/deliver-verify-adjudicate-rc.patch
#     git rm scripts/deliver-verify-adjudicate-rc.patch
#     go test ./scripts/ -run 'TestQAAdjudication|TestQAReportAuthor' -count=1
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT APPLIED. The L1 delivery runner's GitHub App token carries no
# `workflows` permission — there is no such permission to grant in a `permissions:` block — so
# any change under .github/workflows/ is refused, by `git push` and the Contents API alike:
#
#     refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow
#     `.github/workflows/deliver-verify.yml` without `workflows` permission
#
# Measured on this delivery, not inferred: the code half pushed, the same branch with this hunk
# did not. Same situation and same convention as scripts/deliver-implement-approver.patch (#1790)
# and .github/archon-trigger-anchor.patch (#1675).
#
# THE BUG IS ALREADY FIXED WITHOUT THIS FILE. #1834's primary defect — the adjudicate-only
# re-verify could not find its own round-0 report, so every correction round ended at
# needs-human — is repaired in scripts/qa-review/adjudicator.py, which the runner CAN push:
# `same_login()` compares `github-actions` and `github-actions[bot]` as one actor, so the LIVE
# workflow's short-form QA_REPORT_AUTHOR matches the canonical login REST reports. That ordering
# was deliberate. A fix that lived only in this patch would have left the delivery loop broken
# until a human applied it.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES — both hunks are in the `Run qa-review adjudication` step, and both are
# secondary hardening on top of that fix:
#
#   1. QA_REPORT_AUTHOR: github-actions -> github-actions[bot]. Cosmetic-but-correct: the value
#      becomes the spelling the comment source actually emits, so the restriction reads as what
#      it means rather than relying on the tolerance added above. Either value works now.
#
#   2. `rc=0; python3 ... || rc=$?` in place of a bare `rc=$?` on the following line. THIS IS
#      THE ONE WITH REAL BEHAVIOUR, and it is the defect that hid the primary one for as long as
#      it did. The step runs under the default `bash -e {0}`, and its `set -uo pipefail` does not
#      clear `-e`, so a nonzero `python3` aborted the step AT THE INVOCATION — before the `rc`
#      capture, before the `cat ...err >&2`, and before the `skip`. The intended MISSING reason
#      ("the qa-review adjudicator exited 3 (no prior report to adjudicate, or a failure)") was
#      therefore never posted, and the loop reported only the generic "the verify phase failed or
#      timed out". Reproduced under `bash -e`: the old shape exits 3 printing nothing, the new
#      one prints the adjudicator's stderr and the skip reason and exits 0. The sibling
#      author-gate steps in this same workflow already use the inline `|| rc=$?` form.
#
# ALREADY LANDED, NOT IN THIS PATCH: scripts/deliver_qa_adjudicate_test.go's #1834 guards. Until
# this file is applied they read the step OUT OF THIS FILE (applying the diff to a temp copy), so
# the contract is under test now and the patch is proven to apply cleanly on every CI run. Once
# applied they read deliver-verify.yml directly, and they FAIL if this file is deleted without
# being applied — so neither half can be silently lost. The state CI stays green in is "merged
# and simply never applied": the `-e` masking then persists, which costs a clear diagnostic on a
# failing adjudication but no longer breaks the loop, because the primary fix is live.
#
# Everything below this line is a plain `git diff`; the `#` lines above are ignored by git apply.
diff --git a/.github/workflows/deliver-verify.yml b/.github/workflows/deliver-verify.yml
index af16c6df..d57c38d9 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/deliver-verify.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/deliver-verify.yml
@@ -1276,7 +1276,14 @@ jobs:
           # this PUBLIC repository from supplying its own report-shaped comment with an empty
           # Items-to-fix section and clearing every outstanding finding. A mismatch is fail-closed:
           # the adjudicator finds no report, emits no verdict, and the gate stops for a human.
-          QA_REPORT_AUTHOR: github-actions
+          #
+          # Spelled CANONICALLY, with the `[bot]` suffix, because that is what the comment source
+          # reports: scripts/deliver-trusted-comments.sh reads REST (#1806), whose App logins carry
+          # the suffix. The short form here is what broke every re-verify in #1834 — fail-closed
+          # made it a silent needs-human on every multi-round delivery rather than a visible error.
+          # adjudicator.py now compares the two spellings as one actor (same_login), so this value
+          # is the canonical one rather than the only one that works.
+          QA_REPORT_AUTHOR: github-actions[bot]
           # The commit `Validate the target PR` recorded and CI verified, for the reason the
           # round-0 branch uses it: a bare FETCH_HEAD would follow whatever fetched last.
           HEAD_SHA: ${{ steps.target.outputs.sha }}
@@ -1294,12 +1301,21 @@ jobs:
 
           # stderr is captured rather than piped: it carries the verdict line AND the tool-loop
           # diagnostics, both of which belong in the job log too.
+          #
+          # `|| rc=$?` and not a bare `rc=$?` on the next line (#1834): this step runs under the
+          # default `bash -e {0}`, and `set -uo pipefail` above does NOT clear `-e`. A nonzero
+          # python3 therefore aborted the step AT THE INVOCATION — before the capture, before the
+          # `cat`, and before the `skip` — so the intended MISSING reason was never posted and the
+          # loop reported only the generic "the verify phase failed or timed out". That is how a
+          # deterministic exit 3 went undiagnosed. The sibling author-gate steps use this same
+          # inline form for the same reason.
+          rc=0
           python3 scripts/qa-review/adjudicator.py \
             --worktree "$WORKTREE" --no-exec \
             --pr "$PR" --repo "$REPO" \
             --out "$RUNNER_TEMP/qa-report.md" \
-            2> "$RUNNER_TEMP/qa-adjudication.err"
-          rc=$?
+            2> "$RUNNER_TEMP/qa-adjudication.err" \
+            || rc=$?
           cat "$RUNNER_TEMP/qa-adjudication.err" >&2 || true
           [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] \
             || skip "the qa-review adjudicator exited $rc (no prior report to adjudicate, or a failure)"
